# deps / builds
node_modules/
.next/
.next/standalone/
.vercel
coverage/
venv-calib/
# ANY virtualenv, not just the one we happened to name. `.venv-ml/` went unignored and the
# nightly `git add -A` backup swept 8,005 files / 356 MB into the repo on 2026-10-19 (.git hit
# 861 MB). The >100 MB per-file guard cannot see a directory full of small files, so a
# directory-level mistake like this is invisible to it — the ignore rule is the only defence.
.venv*/
rust-sidecar/target/
*.pyc
__pycache__/

# logs
logs/

# secrets — never commit
.env
.env.local
*.pem
*.key

# databases (runtime state)
*.db
*.db-journal
prisma/dev.db*

# large runtime data (L2 book history — 700MB+; keep the small research JSONL)
data/l2/
# pinned-market quote-event tape (v62) — same append-only, regenerable-from-the-venue character
data/l2-events/

# C-200 print-through: tape cache is refetchable, the watcher state churns every tick
data/c200-tape/
data/c200-intent-watch-state.json

# shadow marker attempt ledger (2026-10-10, card t_81d0a4cb): per-candidate
# attempt stamps for the rotating settlement walks. Rewritten on every hourly
# marker run (the walk stamps ~300 rows/run), so it is runtime state like the
# c200 watcher above — it must survive a process restart, which is the file on
# disk, not a git commit.
data/shadow-attempt-state.json

# large derived datasets — regenerate with scripts/build-decision-dataset.py (read-only DB read)
data/decision-dataset.csv

# archive-analysis parquet extracts (2026-09-28, tuning review #38 rec 1): the
# daily git backup committed a 137 MB asset-labels parquet, which GitHub rejects
# outright (>100 MB per file, pre-receive hook) — the push failed on 09-26 and
# 09-27 and the failure is invisible outside logs/cron/copybot-git-backup.log.
# These are derived extracts rebuilt by the archive scripts: keep them off GitHub.
data/archive-analysis/*.parquet

# large binaries / regenerable assets
de421.bsp
*.bsp
*.tsbuildinfo

# kanban worker worktrees — the gateway dispatcher spawns one per repo-editing card
# (`.worktrees/<card-id>`). Without this rule the nightly `git add -A` backup stages
# every worker's tree into main; branches are the review surface, not the worktree dirs.
.worktrees/

# GDELT bulk (keyless 15-min export) shadow rows — high volume (~26k rows/day, ~2 MB/day
# gzipped) and NOT a decision input: daily gzip append under data/gdelt-bulk/. Keeping it out
# of the repo protects the nightly backup job's 95 MB per-file guard.
data/gdelt-bulk/

# Kalshi venue tape (2026-10-12, complementary-Kalshi workstream option A).
# USER OVERRIDE 2026-10-12 ("override both"): the tape artifacts are TRACKED — the
# captured history is the un-recoverable asset and a local-only copy is one disk failure
# from gone. What makes that safe is TIME ROTATION in the capture script, not luck:
# prints are gzipped per HOUR (~1 MB each), bars roll to bars-<date>.jsonl.gz on the date
# change (~3 MB), and coverage.jsonl is one ~1 KB row per 15-min tick. Measured
# 2026-10-12: ~22 MB/day of prints + ~11 MB/day of bars uncompressed, so no single
# tracked file approaches the nightly backup's 95 MB per-file guard (or GitHub's 100 MB
# hard limit) the way the old whole-day naming would have.
# ONLY the volatile cursor is excluded: state.json is ~0.9 MB, rewritten on every tick,
# and is a resumable position rather than data (losing it costs at most one window's
# dedupe/last-minute bookkeeping, which the next run rebuilds by re-scanning).
data/kalshi-tape/state.json

# Macro-lane quotes are tracked too (one row per open market per run, ~3 MB/day): the
# lane's read needs the quote AT a horizon, so thinning them to settlement-only rows
# would delete the study. Settled + news files were already tracked.

# Frozen analysis artefacts: 50 files, ~530 MB, last written 2026-10-10.
# Kept ON DISK (nothing reads them from git); removed from the index so no
# future clone/backup carries them. Already in history — see the backup-scope
# card, a history rewrite is the only way to shrink .git and that is a
# separate, destructive decision.
data/archive-analysis/
